- op: create  # 4f72
  at: '2026-04-07T07:45:38Z'  # 4f72
  by: agent  # 4f72
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4f72
  clock: 1  # 4f72
  nonce: 4f72  # 4f72
  data:  # 4f72
    kind: invariant  # 4f72
    title: "Go receiver-type inference must follow method-call return types"  # 4f72
    status: violated  # 4f72
    priority: 1  # 4f72
    parent:  # 4f72
    tags:  # 4f72
    - analysis_quality  # 4f72
    - cross_language_linkers  # 4f72
    before: []  # 4f72
    duplicate_of: []  # 4f72
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4f72
    pr_ref:  # 4f72
    description: "Source: deep-20260407-030917 prometheus reflect agent. Three observed misresolutions in API.query outgoing edges: qry.Close -> ResourceClient.Close (wrong), warnings.Add -> unsafeLabelAdder.Add (wrong), r.Context -> LazyLoader.Context (wrong, should be http.Request.Context). Receiver type tracking from WI-hikug catches direct type assignments and parameters but not types that flow through return values of method calls or struct field accesses. Affects every Go repo with the .Close()/.Add()/.Set()/.Context() pattern (essentially every Go service). Likely fix: extend _extract_go_var_types to record return types from typed method calls when the caller's type is known. Same issue contaminates symbol_ranking globally because every .Close() call in the codebase pollutes a single ResourceClient.Close with bogus in-degree."  # 4f72
    fields:  # 4f72
      statement: "Go method calls on local variables that hold the return value of another method call (or struct field access) must resolve via the receiver's typed return type, not via global short-name lookup."  # 4f72
      root_cause: "WI-hikug closed with receiver type tracking that handles short var declarations, var declarations, function parameters, and method receivers — but it does not propagate types through method-call return values. Example from prometheus deep-20260407-030917 web/api/v1/api.go:502-569 API.query: qry, err := ng.NewInstantQuery(...) should give qry the type promql.Query (return type of QueryEngine.NewInstantQuery). Then qry.Close() should resolve to promql.Query.Close. Instead it resolves to ResourceClient.Close from a different package. Same pattern for warnings.Add and r.Context."  # 4f72
- op: discuss  # b24a
  at: '2026-04-07T07:53:57Z'  # b24a
  by: agent  # b24a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b24a
  clock: 2  # b24a
  nonce: b24a  # b24a
  message: "Verified by reading go.py:1248-1557. The receiver type tracker has 4 patterns: (1) short_var_declaration via _type_from_rhs which only handles literals (Server{}, &Server{}) and constructor naming (NewFoo() -> Foo); (2) var_spec; (3) parameter_declaration; (4) method_declaration. The misresolutions in API.query are caused by gaps: (a) qry := ng.NewInstantQuery(...) — _type_from_constructor_call infers 'InstantQuery' (strip New + capital match), but the actual return type is Query (interface). qry.Close then can't find InstantQuery.Close and falls back to global lookup -> ResourceClient.Close. (b) warnings := q.Warnings() — not a constructor naming pattern, so no type inferred at all. (c) r.Context() — r is a parameter with type *http.Request, parameter_declaration handler stores it as 'http.Request', but http.Request methods are not in source; resolver falls back globally. The structural fix requires a pre-pass that records each function/method declaration's return type into a registry, then when processing qry := bar.NewInstantQuery() looks up NewInstantQuery's actual return type instead of guessing from the name. This is a substantial architectural addition (cross-file pre-pass + new registry + new resolution path), not a small extension to _extract_go_var_types. Recommending P2 not P1 since the false targets are largely cosmetic (centrality pollution, slice noise) rather than a correctness regression vs prior sessions."  # b24a
