- op: discuss  # d378
  at: '2026-04-07T10:08:52Z'  # d378
  by: agent  # d378
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d378
  clock: 8  # d378
  nonce: d378  # d378
  message: "Investigated cross-language analogs via Explore agent. Verdict: this gap affects at least 8 statically-typed languages in structurally similar ways. The fix should NOT be Go-specific.\n\nLANGUAGES BY CATEGORY:\n\n**FULLY AFFECTED (same gap as Go)** — return-type-through-method-call resolution missing:\n- Java\n- Kotlin (return type tracking exists but doesn't propagate through method-call returns)\n\n**PARTIALLY AFFECTED** — some related infrastructure exists but return-value chaining missing:\n- C++ (explicit type declarations exist, but tree-sitter-cpp limits AST reconstruction; STL guard prevents false positives by enumeration, not by tracking)\n- C# (docstring mentions 'chained field type resolution' but implementation delegates without explicit return-value tracking)\n- Rust (struct field types enable self.field.method() resolution, but not return-value chaining for local variables)\n- PHP (optional type annotations exist but analyzer doesn't build return-type registries)\n- Scala (statically typed but no return-type tracking in analyzer)\n\n**NOT APPLICABLE** (dynamic typing makes the question incoherent without annotations):\n- Python, Ruby, JavaScript, Lua — receiver type is fundamentally unknowable at parse time without explicit annotations. These should be assessed separately: support restricted to annotated code, or gap is expected.\n\n**INSUFFICIENT TYPE TRACKING** (no tracking infrastructure to extend):\n- Swift, Kotlin (no full type tracking), Groovy, Dart\n\nCROSS-LINKER CHECK: type_hierarchy.py builds inheritance maps from extends/implements edges but operates post-analysis. It does not address the return-value type registry need.\n\nDESIGN IMPLICATION: a Go-specific fix to _extract_go_var_types would solve only 1 of ~8 affected languages and waste the architectural opportunity. The right approach is a shared pre-pass in the core analyzer framework:\n\n1. **Function-return-type registry pre-pass**: walk all symbols once, extract return types from declarations (signatures for typed languages, type hints for dynamic). Store as registry: dict[symbol_id] → return_type.\n2. **Per-language signature extractors**: each statically-typed analyzer contributes a small extractor that pulls return types from its language's syntax. ~30-50 lines per language.\n3. **Per-language consumers**: each analyzer's var_types inference gets one new branch — when processing 'x := obj.method()', look up method's return type in registry instead of guessing from name.\n\nThis becomes ADR-worthy because it's a cross-cutting architectural addition. Suggest scoping it as:\n- Phase 1: Java + Go (highest-value statically typed languages with most repos in the bakeoff pool, both already have receiver type tracking infrastructure)\n- Phase 2: Kotlin + C# + Rust (existing infrastructure; smaller delta)\n- Phase 3: C++ + Scala + PHP (no existing infrastructure; larger delta)\n- Phase 4: type-annotated Python (existing PEP 484 type hints make this tractable)\n\nANSWER TO YOUR DIRECT QUESTION ('might analogous situations arise for other languages besides golang?'): **Yes, at least 7 other languages have the same structural gap**. The fix should be cross-language. INV-dihos should be either (a) re-scoped as a meta-invariant covering all statically-typed languages, or (b) kept Go-specific and have sibling items created for each affected language. I recommend (a) for cleaner tracker organization."  # d378
