- op: discuss  # 254d
  at: '2026-04-07T18:15:55Z'  # 254d
  by: agent  # 254d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 254d
  clock: 10  # 254d
  nonce: 254d  # 254d
  message: "ANSWERS:\n\n(1) AUGMENT ADR-0006, do NOT create a new ADR. The right home is already there: ADR-0006 'Variable Type Inference for Method Call Resolution' covers this topic and explicitly lists return type tracking among its sources (§ Type Inference Sources, item 4) — but the existing implementation table tracks 'Return' as a single boolean for a much narrower property: 'function declares a return type annotation, so we can use it when assigning return value'. The gap INV-dihos surfaces is the next architectural layer: actually USING those declarations as a global registry, so 'qry := ng.NewInstantQuery(...)' resolves the type of qry by looking up NewInstantQuery's return type rather than name-guessing. Concretely, the augmentation should:\n  - Add a new column 'Return-value chained' to the implementation table (Python ✅, Java ✅, others depends on phase rollout)\n  - Add a 'Return-Type Registry Pre-Pass' section between current §Decision and §Consequences. This is the cross-cutting architectural change.\n  - Refresh Future Work to call out the Phase 1-4 plan from my prior message.\n  - Cross-reference WI-hukoh (the dataflow option 2/3 follow-on) since both items concern 'edge metadata at link time vs node-state at BFS time' tradeoffs.\n\n(2) HYBRID YAML+CODE is the right answer to your second question, AND it lines up with the existing dataflow.py / *.yaml split:\n\nYAML (declarative, ~10-30 lines per language):\n  - Which tree-sitter node types are function/method declarations (e.g. Go: function_declaration, method_declaration; Java: method_declaration, constructor_declaration)\n  - Which child field/position holds the return type (e.g. Java: child name='type'; Go: result child)\n  - Which child holds the receiver/method-name (for binding the registry key)\n  - Generic-stripping regex (uniform across languages: '<.*>' → '')\nThis is sufficient for ~70% of method declarations in most languages.\n\nPython (escape hatches, ~30-50 lines per language):\n  - Tuple return types (Go: '(promql.Query, error)' — pick the non-error result)\n  - Result/Option unwrapping (Rust: 'Result<promql.Query, _>' — unwrap to T)\n  - Cross-package type qualification (Go: 'promql.Query' vs bare 'Query'; needs import-aware resolution)\n  - Receiver type binding for methods (Go: '(qe *QueryEngine) NewInstantQuery(...) -> Query' must register as 'QueryEngine.NewInstantQuery' → Query, not just 'NewInstantQuery' → Query)\n\nThe dataflow.yaml precedent is exactly the model: simple positional rules in YAML, complex per-language logic in dataflow.py annotators. A new 'signatures.py' module would walk all language analyzers' tree-sitter outputs once, apply YAML positional rules + per-language overrides, and emit a 'function_signatures: dict[symbol_id, ReturnType]' artifact attached to the AnalysisRun. Each language analyzer's existing var_types code then gains one new resolution path: when it sees 'x := obj.method()', look up obj's class.method in the signature registry and assign x that return type.\n\nARCHITECTURAL FIT: this is structurally identical to dataflow's two-tier model (Tier 1 automatic via base.py annotate_dataflow + Tier 2 explicit linker overrides). The signature registry would be Tier 1 automatic (per-language signature extraction) + Tier 2 explicit (cross-package type qualifier resolution from imports).\n\nNEXT STEP: a separate WI item to propose the augmented ADR-0006 § 'Return-Type Registry Pre-Pass' as a new tracker entry. Should I create it now, or do you want to first endorse the YAML+code split? If endorsed, I'll draft the ADR augmentation and propose Phase 1 (Java + Go) as the first concrete WI."  # 254d
