- op: discuss  # ce4c
  at: '2026-04-07T21:27:36Z'  # ce4c
  by: agent  # ce4c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ce4c
  clock: 12  # ce4c
  nonce: ce4c  # ce4c
  message: "ADR-0006 augmentation merged in PR #2771. New §'Return-Type Registry Pre-Pass' between Decision and Consequences covers: architecture diagram, two-tier YAML+Python design (mirrors dataflow.yaml + dataflow.py per ADR-0015), receiver-binding rule ({ReceiverType}.{MethodName} not bare {MethodName}), cross-linker check (type_hierarchy + dataflow are consumers, integration is Phase 5), and full four-phase rollout. Implementation table now has a 'Chained' column with ❌ + Phase N annotations for every statically-typed analyzer.\n\nPHASE 1 — Java + Go (highest-value, both already track receiver types in var_types). Deliverables:\n\n(1a) New 'signatures.py' module under packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/. Builds the global function-signature registry once per AnalysisRun by walking every analyzer's tree-sitter output, attached to the run context as 'function_signatures: dict[str, str]'. Tier 1 = positional rules from each '<lang>.yaml'; Tier 2 = per-language Python escape hatches dispatched by edge.language.\n\n(1b) New 'signatures' YAML section in packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/configs/go.yaml: function_node_types (function_declaration, method_declaration), receiver_field (receiver), name_field (name), result_field (result), generic_strip_pattern ('<.*>').\n\n(1c) New 'signatures' YAML section in packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/configs/java.yaml: function_node_types (method_declaration, constructor_declaration), receiver_field null (methods belong to enclosing class_declaration — must be looked up by walking ancestors), name_field (name), return_type_field (type), generic_strip_pattern ('<.*>').\n\n(1d) Python escape hatches in signatures.py go_extract():\n  - Tuple return — pick non-error result. 'func NewInstantQuery(...) (Query, error)' → register Query, drop error.\n  - Cross-package qualifier strip — 'promql.Query' → 'Query' (matches symbol storage).\n  - Receiver binding — '(qe *QueryEngine) M()' registers as 'QueryEngine.M' not bare 'M'. Strip pointer star and package qualifier from receiver type.\n\n(1e) Python escape hatches in signatures.py java_extract():\n  - Constructor handling — register 'ClassName' (not 'ClassName.ClassName' or '<init>') under the enclosing class.\n  - Generic erasure — covered by Tier 1 generic_strip_pattern.\n\n(1f) Wire registry into go.py and java.py 'var_types' fill loop. Add a single new resolution path: 'when processing x := obj.method() and obj's type is known via existing rules, look up {obj_type}.{method_name} in function_signatures and assign x that return type'. Place AFTER existing constructor/parameter/field rules so they retain precedence.\n\n(1g) Regression tests: tests/test_go_signature_registry.py and tests/test_java_signature_registry.py. Each test uses a small in-process tree-sitter parse (no real repo) covering: simple return type, receiver-bound method, tuple-return-with-error (Go), generic erasure, cross-package qualifier (Go), constructor (Java).\n\n(1h) Bakeoff validation: re-run prometheus deep-bakeoff after Phase 1. Forward slice from cmd/promtool/main.go:queryRange should walk into promql.Engine.exec → evalSampleVector. Compare iter-NNN slice.0 node count against the prior iteration to confirm chained receiver resolution actually grew the slice.\n\nPHASE 2 — Kotlin + C# (existing var_types infrastructure). Smaller delta because Tier 2 escape hatches are well-understood:\n  - Kotlin nullable suffix: 'Foo?' → 'Foo'.\n  - C# async unwrapping: 'Task<T>' → 'T', 'ValueTask<T>' → 'T', 'IAsyncEnumerable<T>' → 'T'.\n  - Same wiring pattern as Phase 1 (kotlin.py + csharp.py var_types fill loops).\n  - Bakeoff repos: kserve (Kotlin), aspnet (C#).\n\nPHASE 3 — Rust + C++ + Scala (no existing var_types infrastructure — prerequisite work). Largest delta. Each language needs:\n  - Constructor + parameter extraction first (the existing 'Maybe' entries in §Analyzers Without Type Tracking).\n  - Tier 2 escape hatches: Rust 'Result<T,E>' / 'Option<T>' unwrap, 'impl Trait' opacity (leave unregistered);  # ce4c
    C++ pointer/reference strip ('Query*' → 'Query', 'Query&' → 'Query'); Scala implicits deferred to a follow-on item.\n  - Bakeoff repos: alertmanager Rust workspace, any LLVM-style C++ repo, any Spark/Akka Scala monorepo.\n\nPHASE 4 — Type-annotated Python. Promote py.py:1648's local return-type extraction to a global registry entry. Untyped Python remains n/a. Bakeoff repo: kserve (mixed-typed Python, well-suited because its public API uses PEP 484 throughout).\n\nPHASE 5 (follow-on) — cross-linker integration. type_hierarchy.py fallback for ambiguous receiver types in the registry; dataflow.py refinement for chained method-call access_mode. Optional, only after Phases 1-4 are validated against deep-bakeoff metrics.\n\nNEXT STEP: shall I create WI items for Phases 1-5 now (one WI each, with Phase 1 as todo_hard since it has the clearest signal and Phases 2-5 as todo_soft awaiting Phase 1 bakeoff validation)? Or do you want to first review the ADR augmentation in PR #2771 and revise the phase boundaries before I trackerize?"  # ce4c
