- op: discuss  # 34cf
  at: '2026-04-09T10:34:20Z'  # 34cf
  by: agent  # 34cf
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 34cf
  clock: 17  # 34cf
  nonce: 34cf  # 34cf
  message: "Investigation of the 708 chained_call_unresolved edges in prometheus: these are NOT a failure of the return-type registry. They are INLINE chained calls (e.g. ng.NewInstantQuery().Exec()) where the receiver is itself a call_expression. The chained-call ambiguity guard at go.py:2108-2127 correctly emits these as unresolved because there is no intermediate variable to type.\n\nThe return-type registry (WI-kuroj) works for VARIABLE ASSIGNMENT cases: 'qry := ng.NewInstantQuery(...)' puts 'Query' into var_types['qry'], enabling 'qry.Exec()' to resolve via typed_receiver_call. But inline chains bypass var_types entirely.\n\nTo resolve inline chains, a new mechanism is needed: when the receiver is a call_expression, extract the callee method name, look it up in the return-type registry, and use the return type to qualify the outer method. This is a Phase 2 enhancement, not a Phase 1 bug.\n\nSummary: the return-type registry IS populating correctly (confirmed by 15 unit tests passing with grammar-gated assertions). The 708 unresolved edges are inline chained calls that the current architecture doesn't handle. Phase 1 handles variable-assignment chaining; inline chaining is future work."  # 34cf
